sec.p:
The mean activity of ROIs during each trial type was modeled as a linear combination of three task variables: sensory cue, choice, and outcome (Chen et al., 2017; Yang et al., 2022). At each timepoint t in the trial, three task-variable selectivity CDs were defined as:
(2)
s(t)=12[(xCR(t)+xIR(t))−(xCL(t)+xIL(t))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{s}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CR}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IR}}(t)\right)-\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CL}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IL}}(t)\right)\right] \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
c(t)=12[(xCR(t)+xIL(t))−(xCL(t)+xIR(t))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{c}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CR}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IL}}(t)\right)-\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CL}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IR}}(t)\right)\right] \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(4)
o(t)=12[(xCR(t)+xCL(t))−(xIR(t)+xIL(t))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{o}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CR}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{CL}}(t)\right)-\left(\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IR}}(t)+\mathbf{x}_{\mathrm{IL}}(t)\right)\right] \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where xk(t) denotes the trial-averaged activity for trial type k at time t. For each timepoint, selectivity CDs were then orthogonalized by QR decomposition in the order O(t), c(t), s(t).

To quantify the correlation between CDs across training epochs, we calculated the cosine similarity between the mean-subtracted CDs, which was equivalent to the Pearson correlation. Empirical measurements of correlation will be systematically lower than true underlying correlations due to noise. To place across-epoch correlations into context, we estimated a maximum observable correlation (i.e., correlation “ceiling”) based on the within-epoch reliability of the CD estimate. Within-epoch reliability was defined as the correlation between two CDs derived from a random 50/50 trial-level split of the epoch. The maximum observable cross-epoch correlation was defined as:
(6)
rceiling(t)=r1(t)⋅r2(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\text {ceiling }}(t)=\sqrt{r^{1}(t) \cdot r^{2}(t)}, \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where r1(t) and r2(t) denote the within-epoch correlations of training epoch 1 and training epoch 2. The cross-epoch correlation (rcross(t)) was then normalized by this correlation ceiling to obtain a reliability-corrected correlation estimate:
(7)
rratio(t)=rcross(t)rceiling(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} r_{\text {ratio }}(t)=\frac{r_{\text {cross }}(t)}{r_{\text {ceiling }}(t)} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The characteristic response time (tresponse) was then used to calculate the Responsiveness and Net Selectivity for each ROI:


Responsiveness=xCR(tresponse)+xCL(tresponse)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text { Responsiveness }=x_{\mathrm{CR}}\left(t_{\text {response }}\right)+x_{\mathrm{CL}}\left(t_{\text {response }}\right)$$\end{document}



and


    Net Selectivity=xCR(tresponse)−xCL(tresponse).
    \begin{document}$$\displaystyle =x_{\mathrm{CR}}\left(t_{\text {response }}\right)-x_{\mathrm{CL}}\left(t_{\text {response }}\right)$$\end{document}
